<h2>Cancellation: win-back sequence</h2>
<p>Client gives notice. The 12-month minimum has been met. Run this:</p>

<h3>Step 1: same-day acknowledgment</h3>
<p>"Got it. Before I action it, can we hop on a 15-minute call so I can understand why?" Most cancellations get a yes here. The call is the leverage.</p>

<h3>Step 2: the call</h3>
<p>Listen. Ask:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Has something changed in the business?" (often the real reason)</li>
  <li>"Is there something I have not delivered well enough?"</li>
  <li>"If I could do ONE thing to make this stay worth it, what would it be?"</li>
</ul>
<p>Take notes. Do not pitch. Listen.</p>

<h3>Step 3: written response within 24 hours</h3>
<p>Two paths.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>If retention is salvageable:</strong> propose a specific, scoped action that addresses the concern. Often a small upsell or a re-prioritized roadmap. No discount on price.</li>
  <li><strong>If retention is not salvageable:</strong> confirm the offboarding (per <code>/sop/offboarding/</code>) gracefully, document the reason, and ask if they would be open to a referral if the next provider does not work out.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 4: post-mortem</h3>
<p>Within 7 days of the cancellation effective date:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Document the reason in the churn log (Google Sheet).</li>
  <li>Categorize: business closed, switched provider, in-house, dissatisfied with my work, dissatisfied with the deliverable, price.</li>
  <li>If it is the last three, identify the SOP that needs updating.</li>
</ul>

<h2>KPIs</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Annual retention &ge; 90% on monthly clients.</li>
  <li>1-2 upsells per quarter from the existing book.</li>
  <li>Lifetime value &gt; 36 months on the median monthly client.</li>
  <li>Win-back rate &ge; 20% on cancellations where the post-call offer is made.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>QBRs slip past the schedule.</strong> Block them on the calendar at month 5.5 and again at month 11.5. Without the calendar block they do not happen.</li>
  <li><strong>Upsell pitched without context.</strong> The QBR earns the right to mention the upsell catalog. Cold upsell emails do not work.</li>
  <li><strong>Win-back sequence skipped.</strong> Even when the answer is going to be no, the call gives me information about WHY, which improves the SOP for everyone else.</li>
</ul>
